{
  "esm": "0.8.0",
  "metadata": {
    "name": "latlon3d_advection_3d",
    "description": "INTEGRATION driver: full 3-D advection on the latlon3d grid, u_t = -(omega_deg D(u,lon) + v_deg D(u,lat) + w_lev D(u,lev)) + S, composing ALL THREE directional rules at once — grids/latlon3d/rules/upwind1_D_lon_periodic (1st-order upwind, periodic), central_D_lat_zero_grad_bc (2nd-order centered, zero-gradient poles), and central_D_lev_zero_grad_bc (2nd-order centered nonuniform, zero-gradient top/bottom) — to demonstrate that the three rules fire independently on the same [lon,lat,lev] field and assemble into one RHS with no match conflict. Forced-steady manufactured solution u*(lon,lat,z) = sin((pi/180) lon) * cos^4((pi/180) lat) * cos(pi z): periodic in lon (period 360, closes the zonal circle at NLON dlon_deg=360), zero meridional gradient at the poles (cos^4 vanishes to 3rd order — compatible with the zero-gradient pole rows), and zero vertical gradient at the top/bottom (cos(pi z), z in [0,1] — compatible with the zero-gradient lev closures). The matching steady forcing S = omega_deg u*_lon + v_deg u*_lat + w_lev u*_z (each analytic per-degree / per-z derivative) makes u* a continuous steady state, so the simulated field drifts only by the combined discrete truncation of the three operators (dominated by the 1st-order upwind lon term). Fixed resolution NLON=36, NLAT=19, NLEV=10 (GEOS-Chem 4x5-like horizontal, a 10-level column) edge-closed at the import edges; dlon_deg=10, dlat_deg=10 as literals; the vertical uses the grid Design-B edge array pe (quadratic stretch) -> zc. This is a single-resolution SIMULATION gate (the per-direction ORDER claims are proven separately by the three latlon3d convergence cases); it asserts the ic wiring is exact at t=0 and the combined drift stays bounded at t=T.",
    "license": "MIT",
    "tags": [
      "esd:problem",
      "grid:latlon3d",
      "equation:advection",
      "mms"
    ],
    "references": [
      {
        "citation": "Lin, S.-J., & Rood, R. B. (1996). Multidimensional flux-form semi-Lagrangian transport schemes. Monthly Weather Review, 124(9), 2046-2070.",
        "doi": "10.1175/1520-0493(1996)124<2046:MFFSLT>2.0.CO;2"
      },
      {
        "citation": "Roache, P. J. (2002). Code verification by the method of manufactured solutions. J. Fluids Eng. 124(1), 4-10.",
        "doi": "10.1115/1.1436090"
      }
    ]
  },
